\tag{fdip}
\title{Further constructions and examples}
\date{2026-10-30}

\p{Operator products, support gradings and finite-state expectations
provide complementary examples of the algebra used in discrete
induction. The constructions below also isolate coefficient and
symmetry phenomena that can be read independently of the
[main Gaussian and arithmetic discussion](fdip-0001) and its
[reference prerequisites](fdip-003Z).}

\ul{
\li{[A shifted product rule](fdip-0009)}
\li{[The half-turn in characteristic two](fdip-000N)}
\li{[Composition and field multiplication](fdip-0011)}
\li{[The endpoint product identity](fdip-0012)}
\li{[Shift and multiplication operators](fdip-0013)}
\li{[Internal parity grading](fdip-001J)}
\li{[Parity of field endomorphisms](fdip-001K)}
\li{[Adjoints preserve the chosen parity](fdip-001L)}
\li{[Parity and adjoint on the four-cycle](fdip-001M)}
\li{[Observables on a finite configuration set](fdip-0022)}
\li{[Finite functionals and their weights](fdip-0023)}
\li{[Square positivity need not imply real Hermiticity](fdip-0025)}
\li{[Finite states are probability weights](fdip-0026)}
\li{[Finite-map pullback and fiber weights](fdip-0028)}
\li{[A nonuniform law on sixteen cycle configurations](fdip-0029)}
\li{[What zero incidence and scalar signs forget](fdip-003C)}
\li{[Support parity, group degree and sign sectors](fdip-003P)}
\li{[The complete sector law of the alternating four-cycle](fdip-003S)}
\li{[The half-turn commutant permits mixing within a sector](fdip-003T)}
